<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Rome</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> is the idea that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> succeeded Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> as the centre of true <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the last Christian empire. It was formulated in the early sixteenth century in letters of the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philotheus-of-pskov">Philotheus of Pskov</a>, against the background of the fall of Constantinople in 1453 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>'s marriage to the Byzantine princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-palaiologina">Sophia Palaiologina</a>. Though first a religious warning, it was later read as a statement of Russian imperial destiny.</p></div>
